nd their activity is steady throughout, so neither volume, popularity, nor time-ignored connectivity explains the pattern. The turnover is in which member speaks, not in whom the group talks to."}, "raw_final_response": "All evidence gathered. Key result:\n\n**The group's contact set is essentially invariant over time.** What rotates is *which member* is active \u2014 node 384 fires only in bin 1, 716/946/948 in bins 1\u20132, 1116/1121 in bins 3\u20134, 1304/1464/1635/1740/1955 in bins 4\u20136 (steps 03, 10\u201312). But every member, in whatever bin it is active, contacts the *same* ~30-node core {98, 117, 204, 205, 258, 292, 295, 379, 537, 543, 762, 927, 964, 1020, 1045, 1106, 1123, 1155, 1179, 1184, 1247, 1257, 1283, 1294, 1297, 1410, 1460, 1638, 1848, 1950} \u2014 identical for 716 at t\u224836k (step_26), 946/948 at t\u224841k (steps 14\u201315), 1116 at t\u224846k (step_22), 1304 at t\u224851k (step_23), 1464/1635 at t\u224856k (steps 21, 24), and 1740/1955 at t\u224861k (steps 19\u201320).\n\nAggregating the group before vs. after any candidate change point therefore yields nearly complete overlap (~30 shared, union ~35 \u2192 dissimilarity \u2248 0.14, step_30), far below the 0.6 threshold. The only variation is idiosyncratic single-node extras (1351, 1509, 1986, 439, 382).\n\n**Confounder controls:** the group's event volume is flat (147\u2013224 per bin, step_03), so this is not a volume artifact. The core targets are not global hubs \u2014 they carry only ~37\u201338 events and 13\u201314 partners each (step_28), and are not in the top-active list (step_27, dominated by the group members themselves plus unrelated 1509/1777). Core-target activity is steady across all six bins (25\u201338 events, step_29), so no old-set-dies/new-set-arrives pattern exists on the target side either.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_459f9d4b\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [384, 716, 946, 948, 1116, 1121, 1304, 1464, 15